TI  - The Clinical Utility of Real-Time Patient-Reported Outcomes in Cell Therapy 
      Recipients.
PG  - 1047.e1-1047.e12
LID - S2666-6367(25)01393-4 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jtct.2025.08.018 [doi]
AB  - The use of patient-reported outcome measures (PROMs) can improve a patient's 
      clinical course by decreasing the incidence and severity of both physical and 
      emotional treatment-related toxicities, uncover unmet patient needs, and assist 
      in patients' shared decision-making. The objective of this study was to examine 
      the feasibility and utility of using electronically captured "real time" 
      agenda-setting questions and PROMs at the time of the patient's clinic visit. We 
      designed a prospective observational study that employed a previsit questionnaire 
      (PVQ) that included agenda-setting questions and the Patient-Reported Outcomes 
      Measurement Information System 29-item profile survey, with results incorporated 
      into a clinical decision support dashboard embedded within the patients' 
      electronic medical record. The real-time results within the dashboard were used 
      during each patient's clinic visit to encourage and support conversations between 
      patients and their transplant clinician. Two hundred and thirty-seven consecutive 
      cellular therapy recipients (allogeneic stem cell transplant, n = 98; autologous 
      transplant, n = 106; or chimeric antigen receptor T cell, n = 33) seen between 
      April 2022 and January 2025 were included. Over the study period, PVQ completion 
      rates reached 80%, with an average of 60% weekly. The majority of patients (82%; 
      n = 162) reported that the information in the dashboard helped them talk to their 
      clinician about what mattered most, and 78% of patients (n = 154) noted that the 
      dashboard helped them make healthcare decisions. Each of the transplant 
      clinicians (n = 9) believed that the information gathered from the PVQ was 
      helpful by aiding their discussions with the patient during the clinic visit. 
      When using an internationally recognized and verified process to assess shared 
      decision-making (CollaboRATE), 91% of patients (n = 180) believed that the 
      dashboard assisted them in making future medical decisions. Eighty-nine percent 
      of transplant clinicians agreed that the use of the PVQ aided patients in shared 
      decision-making. Our results demonstrate the feasibility and utility of real-time 
      PROMs and agenda-setting questions embedded within an electronic dashboard for 
      cellular therapy patients. Both clinicians and patients noted marked benefits 
      over the course of the study. These results support the ongoing need for 
      capturing real-time PROMs with advantages to patients and their treating 
      clinicians.
