PMID- 42013220
STAT- Publisher
PB  - Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute (PCORI)
CTI - PCORI Final Research Reports
DP  - 2024 May
BTI - Comparing Ways to Support Communication between Patients and Their Primary Care 
      Providers
AB  - BACKGROUND: Visits with clinicians are at the center of patients' health care 
      experience. Systematic reviews of the preconditions for improving health care 
      delivery have emphasized the importance of patient-physician communication. While 
      numerous efforts to improve patient-physician communication have been attempted, 
      these efforts and their effectiveness have rarely been compared head-to-head. 
      OBJECTIVES: This study was built on a prior PCORI-funded pilot that developed 
      prototypes of interventions to improve patient-physician communication and 
      demonstrated promising results. The present work aimed to engage with patients 
      and health care professionals to further refine and adapt these interventions to 
      a larger number of real-world primary care clinics, evaluate their comparative 
      effectiveness, and identify the strategy with the most potential for sustained 
      impact and replication across health care systems. METHODS: A cluster randomized 
      controlled trial with 3 arms (ClinicalTrials.gov identifier NCT03385512) was 
      conducted in 21 primary care clinics in 3 multispecialty health systems in 3 
      regions of the US, with 4852 patients and 114 primary care clinicians 
      participating. The 3 interventions were (1) communication coaching with in-person 
      standardized patient instructors (High Touch), (2) communication coaching with a 
      mobile app (High Tech), and (3) posters placed on walls of examination rooms to 
      nudge shared decision-making (AskShareKnow [ASK]). The primary outcomes included 
      preselected patient experience with engagement (CollaboRATE) and shared 
      decision-making (Facilitation). Secondary outcomes encompassed patients' 
      confidence in managing their health, patients' intention to follow through with 
      care plans, follow-up 3 months after the visit, and use of services. We also used 
      the Net Promoter Score, a prevailing metric used by many health systems that 
      measures patients' likelihood of recommending a clinician to others. To account 
      for the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic, we used a time variable for national 
      COVID-19 emergency declaration and visit type (in-person vs televisit). A 
      difference-in-differences mixed-effect logistic regression model with random 
      intercepts for clinician was used for analyzing differences in primary outcomes, 
      including superiority tests for High Tech vs ASK and High Touch vs ASK and a 
      noninferiority comparison of High Tech vs High Touch. RESULTS: There was no 
      evidence of significant differences in primary outcomes (CollaboRATE) across 
      intervention arms when assessed using either odds ratios (ORs) or marginal 
      differences in predicted probabilities (Tech vs ASK: OR, 1.013 [95% CI, 
      0.700-1.4640], P = .95, marginal difference, ‒0.021 [95% CI, ‒0.073 to 0.030]; 
      Touch vs ASK, OR, 1.089 [95% CI, 0.749-1.583], P = .66; marginal difference, 
      ‒0.018 [95% CI, ‒0.069 to 0.033]; Tech vs Touch, OR, 0.930 [95% CI, 0.642-1.346], 
      P = .15, marginal difference, ‒0.003 [95% CI, ‒0.057 to 0.052]). High Tech 
      resulted in lower Net Promoter Scores than High Touch (OR, 0.579 [95% CI, 
      0.342-0.978], P = .04; marginal difference, ‒0.056 [95% CI, ‒0.118 to ‒0.119], P 
      = .04). Regarding patients' confidence in managing their health, there were no 
      differences between arms (High Tech vs ASK: mean difference, −0.025 [95% CI, 
      −0.177 to 0.127], P = .75; High Touch vs ASK: mean difference, 0.024 [95% CI, 
      −0.130 to 0.177], P = .77; High Tech vs High Touch: mean difference, −0.048 [95% 
      CI, −0.192 to 0.096], P = .51). After 3 months, patients in High Tech had a 
      significantly lower score than did patients in High Touch (mean difference, 
      −0.176 [95% CI, −0.341 to −0.011]; P = .04), with no differences found in other 
      comparisons. No evidence of difference was found in CollaboRATE between the 
      baseline phase and postintervention. Based on a time-driven activity-based 
      calculation, the mean personnel times required for implementation were 
      approximately 41 hours, 45 hours, and 871 hours for ASK, High Tech, and High 
      Touch, respectively. CONCLUSIONS: The primary outcomes showed no evidence of 
      differential effects of the interventions. Some secondary outcomes (eg, the Net 
      Promoter Score and patients' confidence in their ability to take good care of 
      their health) did show evidence of a superior effect of in-person communication 
      coaching vs the mobile app and no difference with the poster intervention. Future 
      efforts should continue to focus on changing patient and clinician behaviors to 
      make their interactions more patient-centered and conducive for better health 
      outcomes and patient experience. Scientific advancements require the gathering of 
      empirical data to test hypotheses with rigorous methods. Our multisite study 
      shows that it is difficult to exactly translate interventions from a successful 
      pilot study in 1 health system to more diverse clinical practices that vary in 
      ownership and location. As collaborators with health systems, research teams must 
      build strong relationships with leaders and clinicians and balance implementation 
      fidelity with limiting disruptions to clinical workflow. The importance of 
      replications to determine whether efficacy is still sustained beyond the initial 
      pilot project cannot be overstated. LIMITATIONS: This study had multiple 
      limitations. First, the primary outcomes measured by CollaboRATE and Facilitation 
      using topbox or linear form may not be sensitive enough to the intervention to 
      detect a difference. Second, electronic health records as a source of data to 
      examine visit length are subject to technological challenges and contextual 
      variations of visits, resulting in a large amount of variation. Future research 
      efforts should continue to find unobtrusive and reliable ways to measure visit 
      length. Third, the fidelity of implementation might have been limited. The 
      tension between fidelity, flexibility, and pragmatism was present in multiple 
      phases of the study. We operated under a clear intention to be respectful of the 
      challenges faced by clinicians, staff, and clinic managers in their complex 
      real-world practice, particularly with respect to the tremendous stress brought 
      on by the COVID-19 pandemic. Furthermore, the extended and varying lengths of 
      time between intervention implementation and index visits could potentially 
      reduce the likelihood that the physicians would continue to apply the elements of 
      the intervention. Fourth, there was potential selection bias in terms of who 
      decided to participate at the clinic, clinician, and patient levels. There may 
      also have been an issue with the inclusion criteria of having appointments 3 or 
      more days in advance of their visit because patients with more last-minute 
      appointments could have felt differently about their experience. Fifth, the study 
      examined a moderately large number of outcomes, which increases the chance of 
      false-positive results. Given that the outcomes are of interest individually, 
      however, an adjustment for multiple comparisons was not deemed suitable. Finally, 
      no comparison with usual care could be made, which limited our ability to provide 
      more actionable information to health systems in the context of the primary 
      outcomes.
